# document-authority-with-discrepancy-verification

Official documents (approved sketches, specifications) are the single authoritative source of truth for dimensions, torques, and procedures. When a physical artifact (e.g., a part label) appears to contradict the official spec, verify against the physical reality before proceeding. Mandatory checks embedded in official documents must never be removed or bypassed.

## Procedures
- Always retrieve the approved document version (e.g., Sketch v2) before performing a spec-dependent step.
- When a labeled value on a physical part conflicts with the spec, measure the part directly (e.g., ruler for fastener length) before use.
- Use the verified physical measurement—not the misprinted label—when a discrepancy is confirmed.
- Do not remove, skip, or soften any mandatory check referenced in the official document.

## Conventions
- Official sketches and documents supersede memory, informal notes, and part labels.
- Discrepancy between label and spec triggers a physical verification step, not an assumption.
- Mandatory checks are immutable; they survive rework loops and workaround substitutions.

